  m_Name: Brown Spruce Longhorn Beetle
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  bugName: Brown Spruce Longhorn Beetle
  info: 'The brown spruce longhorn beetle (Tetropium fuscum) is native to northern
    and central Europe, Russia and Japan.


    In Canada, it infests spruce trees,
    such as red, white, black and Norway. Except for a small population near Calhoun,
    New Brunswick, the brown spruce longhorn beetle has only been found in Nova Scotia.


    The
    brown spruce longhorn beetle spreads naturally and through the movement of infested
    material such as spruce firewood and spruce logs being shipped to mills.



    It
    prefers and survives better in stressed, weakened and recently fallen spruce
    trees.



    It can infest healthy spruce, but this is rare unless populations
    are high.



    Mature spruce forests under stress from disturbances such as
    drought, defoliation, strong winds are at risk of a brown spruce longhorn beetle
    infestation.



    Once infested, spruce trees die within one to five years.'
  bugImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 53c703a212e3d054c8f290fb3d93e11d, type: 3}
